# Game data

What a game has in it, as data the app reads: for Call of Duty: Warzone, every
weapon a player can equip, grouped the way the game's own loadout screen groups
them. The Auto Actions tab lists these so the owner can pick the weapon a Smart
Action belongs to, and later train the Core to recognise it on screen.

It is app data only. Nothing here is sent to a device.

## The folder

One folder per game, named with the game's pack id (the same id as its file in
`../game-packs/`, so `cod-warzone/` goes with `game-packs/cod-warzone.json`):

```
game-data/
  README.md              this file
  cod-warzone/
    catalog.json         the list (below)
    SOURCES.md           where every fact and every picture came from
    weapons/             one PNG per weapon that has an official picture
```

## catalog.json

```json
{
  "zerostickGameCatalog": 1,
  "game": "cod-warzone",
  "title": "Call of Duty: Warzone",
  "asOf": "2026-09-23",
  "season": "Season 06: The Haunting",
  "integrated": ["Black Ops 7", "Black Ops 6", "Modern Warfare III", "Modern Warfare II"],
  "kind": "weapons",
  "groups": [
    { "id": "assault_rifles", "name": "Assault Rifles", "items": [
      { "id": "assault_rifles.ak_27", "name": "AK-27", "from": "Black Ops 7",
        "image": "weapons/bo7_ak_27.png",
        "sources": ["https://www.callofduty.com/patchnotes/..."],
        "uncertain": null }
    ] }
  ]
}
```

| field | what it is |
|---|---|
| `zerostickGameCatalog` | always `1` |
| `game` | the folder name, the game's pack id |
| `title` | the game's name |
| `asOf` | the date the list was last checked against the game |
| `season` | the season live on that date, as the publisher names it |
| `integrated` | every title whose weapons can be equipped. The first is the premium title the game is integrated with; the rest are older titles kept as Legacy |
| `kind` | what the items are: `weapons` |
| `groups` | the classes, in the game's order. Each has an `id`, a `name` and at least one item |
| `items[].id` | the weapon's permanent id (see Ids) |
| `items[].name` | the weapon's name, spelt the way the publisher spells it |
| `items[].from` | the title it comes from, one of `integrated` |
| `items[].image` | `weapons/<file>.png`, or `null` when there is no official picture |
| `items[].sources` | the official pages that name it and say it is in the game |
| `items[].uncertain` | `null`, or a sentence saying what is not certain and why |
| `items[].fireMode` | optional: `"auto"`, `"burst"`, `"semi"`, `"bolt"` or `"pump"`, how the gun fires (2026-09-25: the owner's rapid fire goes on every burst and semi-auto gun; Black Ops II prints it beside the gun's name) |
| `items[].ammo` | optional: the ammo the gun takes, a list of `"light"`, `"heavy"`, `"energy"`, `"shotgun"`, `"sniper"`, `"arrows"` (2026-10-01, Apex Legends, whose guns each take one type; the C.A.R. SMG takes two). The gun's own page says it under its class. App data only, like the rest |

No other field may appear; `node dashboard/tools/test-game-data.js` refuses one.
The optional top-level fields, in either form, are `tiers` (below) and
`review`.

## Held for review: `review`

A catalog the owner has not approved yet carries a top-level `review`, a
sentence saying what he still has to check:

```json
  "review": "Waiting for the owner's check (D132, D269): names verified on the web, no real frames yet."
```

The app ships the file but treats the game as having no catalog: AI Scripts
for it say "not built yet", nothing can be made against its items, and so
nothing of it reaches a Core (D132: no game goes live for AI Scripts or gun
reading until the owner says so). **Approving a game is deleting its
`review` line** and its name from `HELD` in `test-game-data.js`, in one
commit; the test fails if the two disagree. Held today (D269): Overwatch, VALORANT,
Marvel Rivals, Battlefield 6, ARC Raiders (2026-10-10).

## Rarity tiers: `tiers` (D134)

A game whose weapons come in rarities lists them, in the game's order, from
the most common up:

```json
  "tiers": [
    { "id": "common", "name": "Common", "colour": "#A8A8A8",
      "sources": ["https://www.callofduty.com/blog/2026/03/black-ops-7-black-ops-royale-battle-royale-deep-dive"],
      "note": "Activision says grey; guides from 2020 to 2023 said white." },
    { "id": "legendary", "name": "Legendary", "colour": "#F87001",
      "sources": ["https://www.callofduty.com/blog/2026/03/black-ops-7-black-ops-royale-battle-royale-deep-dive"],
      "note": null }
  ]
```

| field | what it is |
|---|---|
| `tiers[].id` | the tier's permanent id, lowercase a-z0-9_: a Smart Action made for "Legendary" stores it, and a config's tier slots are numbered by the list's order |
| `tiers[].name` | the name the game uses |
| `tiers[].colour` | `#RRGGBB`, the app's display colour for the chip, sampled from the publisher's own images. **The Core never uses it**: it learns each tier's colour from the owner's crops (`RECOGNITION.md`, "Rarity") |
| `tiers[].sources` | the publisher's pages that name the tier and its colour |
| `tiers[].note` | `null`, or a sentence: what is uncertain, or where the tier applies (Warzone's Ultra is Black Ops Royale only) |

At most 8 (the Core's `ZS_RAR_TIERS_MAX`). A tier is never renumbered or
reused, like an item id: a new tier goes at the end, and one the game drops
keeps its place until no config names it. A "tier" need not be a rarity:
Warzone's `loadout` is the colour of the player's own loadout guns, and a
Smart Action made for it runs only on them.

Which catalogs have them: **Warzone**, seven (`cod-warzone/SOURCES.md`,
"Rarity"). **Fortnite** has rarities too (Common, Uncommon, Rare, Epic,
Legendary, Mythic, Exotic, and Special for Sprites), shown on the quickbar
slot rather than beside the name; not listed yet, because the Core's reading
of them needs its own place on screen, which nobody has checked on real
frames. Black Ops 7 multiplayer, Black Ops II and Siege have no weapon rarity.

For Warzone the first ten groups are Black Ops 7's own classes (Assault Rifles
to Melee). The groups named "Legacy ..." are the weapons of Black Ops 6, Modern
Warfare III and Modern Warfare II, which the game keeps behind a single Legacy
tab; those group names are ours, not labels read off the screen (SOURCES.md
says what is and is not confirmed).

## More than one kind: `kinds` (Rainbow Six Siege)

A game whose Auto Actions belong to more than one sort of thing lists each sort
under `kinds`, **instead of** the top-level `kind` and `groups`. Rainbow Six
Siege (`rainbow-six-siege/`) has two: its operators and its guns. The owner
picks the operator he is playing, then that operator's own guns, so every
operator carries its loadout.

```json
{
  "zerostickGameCatalog": 1,
  "game": "rainbow-six-siege",
  "title": "Tom Clancy's Rainbow Six Siege",
  "asOf": "2026-09-23",
  "season": "Year 11 Season 3: Operation Split Fire",
  "integrated": [],
  "kinds": [
    { "kind": "operators", "name": "Operators", "groups": [
      { "id": "attackers", "name": "Attackers", "items": [
        { "id": "attackers.ash", "name": "Ash", "from": "Launch (2015)",
          "image": "operators/ash.png",
          "sources": ["https://www.ubisoft.com/en-us/game/rainbow-six/siege/game-info/operators/ash"],
          "uncertain": null,
          "loadout": { "primary": ["assault_rifles.g36c", "assault_rifles.r4_c"],
                       "secondary": ["handguns.5_7_usg", "handguns.m45_meusoc"] } }
      ] }
    ] },
    { "kind": "weapons", "name": "Guns", "groups": [
      { "id": "assault_rifles", "name": "Assault Rifles", "items": [
        { "id": "assault_rifles.r4_c", "name": "R4-C", "from": null,
          "image": "weapons/r4_c.png",
          "sources": ["https://www.ubisoft.com/en-us/game/rainbow-six/siege/game-info/operators/ash"],
          "uncertain": null, "usedBy": ["attackers.ash", "attackers.ram"] }
      ] }
    ] }
  ]
}
```

What differs from the single-kind form:

| field | what it is |
|---|---|
| `kinds` | replaces `kind` and `groups`: a list, each entry with `kind` (what its items are: `operators`, `weapons`), `name` (the tab the app shows: "Operators", "Guns") and `groups`, exactly as above |
| `integrated` | `[]` for a game with no integrated titles; `from` is then the season the item arrived, as the publisher names it, or `null` |
| `items[].image` | the kind's own folder: `operators/<file>.png` or `weapons/<file>.png`, or `null` |
| `items[].loadout` | the leading kind only, optional: named lists of the other kind's ids, drawn as sections in the order written. Siege's operators have `{ "primary": [gun ids], "secondary": [gun ids] }`, the guns that operator can pick, in the order the publisher's operator page lists them (Blackbeard's `secondary` is empty: he has none). Fortnite's modes have one list, `{ "pool": [gun ids] }`, which the app draws class by class instead (D126). A list may have any lowercase name; an empty one is left out |
| `items[].usedBy` | the carried kind only, optional: the ids of the operators (or modes) who can pick it; `[]` for a Fortnite gun that is vaulted |
| `kinds[].all` | the carried kind only, optional: the name of an entry at the top of the room that lists every item of this kind on its own (Fortnite's `"All guns"`, D126). An item opened there has Smart Actions of its own that run whatever the leading item is. Without it (Siege) a carried item is only ever reached through a leading one |
| `items[].aliases` | any item, either form, optional: other words search should find it by, the names players use (`"SCAR"` for Fortnite's Assault Rifle). Never its own name, never a spelling the publisher uses (that goes in `name`) |

- **Ids are unique across the whole file**, both kinds together. An operator's
  id starts with its side (`attackers.`, `defenders.`), a gun's with its class
  (`assault_rifles.`, `handguns.`); the rest is the slug below. One exception:
  Nøkk is `attackers.nokk`, because ø is a letter, not an accent, and the slug
  rule would make it `n_kk`.
- **`loadout` and `usedBy` always agree**: a gun is in an operator's loadout
  exactly when that operator is in the gun's `usedBy`. Change both together.
- **Pictures** follow D116 as below; for Siege they are Ubisoft's own, from
  ubisoft.com and its image server staticctf.ubisoft.com. An operator's picture
  is the operator's square icon, at most 256 x 256; a gun's is as for Warzone.
- Siege's `SOURCES.md` says where each operator's loadout came from and how to
  update the list when a season adds an operator or moves a gun.

### Modes and All guns (Fortnite, D126)

Fortnite (`fortnite/`) is the same form with a different pair of kinds: its
**modes** lead (Battle Royale, Zero Build, Reload, ...), each with
`loadout: { "pool": [...] }`, the guns in that mode today; its **weapons** are
carried, every gun Fortnite has ever had, vaulted ones included, each with
`usedBy` naming the modes it is in today (`[]` when it is vaulted). The weapons
kind has `"all": "All guns"`, so the app also lists every gun on its own, and a
gun's Smart Actions set there run in every mode, while those set on a mode's
page run only in that mode (and only once the Core has been taught the mode).

- A mode's id starts with its family (`battle_royale.zero_build`,
  `reload.reload`); a gun's with its class, as for Warzone.
- A mode's picture, if Epic publishes one, is in `modes/` and is wide like a
  gun's, at most 480 x 300.
- Fortnite's `SOURCES.md` says where each mode's pool came from, how a mode
  could be recognised on screen, and how to refresh both every update.

## Ids

An id is the group's id, a dot, and the weapon's name made into a slug:
lowercase, accents dropped (`Jäger` to `jager`), apostrophes dropped, every other
run of characters that is not a-z or 0-9 turned into one `_`. `AK-27` in
`assault_rifles` is `assault_rifles.ak_27`; `A.R.C. M1` in `launchers` is
`launchers.a_r_c_m1`. If two weapons in one group have the same name, both get
the title after it: `legacy_melee.pickaxe_bo6` and `legacy_melee.pickaxe_mw2`.

**An id never changes and is never reused**, because the owner's trained
pictures and Auto Actions are stored against it:

- A weapon a patch renames keeps its id and gets the new `name`.
- A weapon that moves group keeps its id, even though the id then starts with
  the old group's name. When the next title is integrated, Black Ops 7's weapons
  move to the Legacy groups with the ids they have now.
- A weapon taken out of the game is deleted from the list, and its id is never
  given to anything else.

## Pictures: official publisher images only (D116)

A weapon's picture must be the publisher's own: for Call of Duty that means
Activision's sites, callofduty.com and its image server imgs.callofduty.com, or
an Activision press kit. For Apex Legends it means Electronic Arts' sites
(ea.com, help.ea.com); `apex-legends/SOURCES.md` says why only eight of its 29
guns have one. Never a fan wiki, Fandom, a loadout site or an image
search. Every picture has a row in the Pictures table of that game's
`SOURCES.md` with its source URL and the date it was fetched, and the test
refuses a picture without one.

Crop to the weapon with its natural shape (guns are wide, about 3:1 or 4:1),
no bigger than 480 x 300, saved as PNG, named `<title>_<slug>.png`
(`bo7_ak_27.png`). Where the publisher has no clean picture, `image` is `null`:
the app shows the class symbol, and later the owner's own trained icon.

## Update the list when a season adds weapons

Warzone adds weapons every few weeks, at each season and each mid-season
"Reloaded" update. For each new weapon:

1. Open that update's Warzone patch notes at
   https://www.callofduty.com/patchnotes. Its **New Weapons** section names each
   weapon and its class ("VMP SMG", "Roc 20mm Special"). The Black Ops 7
   multiplayer notes for the same season write the class in brackets, which
   settles any doubt.
2. Add the weapon at the end of its group in `cod-warzone/catalog.json`, with
   its id (above), `name`, `from`, `sources` (the patch-notes URL) and
   `uncertain: null`.
3. The picture. The same patch notes usually show each weapon's cut-out, at an
   address like
   `https://imgs.callofduty.com/content/dam/atvi/callofduty/cod-touchui/patchnotes/body/bo7/all/<Name>.png`
   (right-click it, Copy image address). Download it, trim it to the weapon and
   save it into `cod-warzone/weapons/`. With Python and Pillow, for a cut-out on
   a transparent background:

   ```
   python -c "from PIL import Image; im=Image.open('VMP.png').convert('RGBA'); im=im.crop(im.getbbox()); im.thumbnail((480,300)); im.save('bo7_vmp.png')"
   ```

   Then set `image` to `weapons/bo7_vmp.png` and add a row to the Pictures table
   in `SOURCES.md`: the file, the weapon, the picture's URL, what kind it is,
   the page that shows it, and today's date. No official picture yet: leave
   `image` as `null` and list it under "No official picture".
4. Set `asOf` to today and `season` to the season now live.
5. Run `node dashboard/tools/test-game-data.js`. It names every mistake.
6. Rebuild the exe and run `node dashboard/tools/sync-mobile.js`, which copies
   this folder into the phone app.

When the next premium title is integrated (Modern Warfare 4, from its Season 1,
expected late 2026): add it to `integrated` (first), give its classes their own
groups in the game's order, and move the Black Ops 7 weapons into the Legacy
groups with their ids unchanged.
